%S A400911 1,9,8,7,19,39,69,68,67,66,38,37,65,99,98,97,96,95,59,58,94,93,57,89,
%T A400911 88,87,86,85,84,83,82,81,49,79,78,77,76,75,74,73,109,155,156,158,159,
%U A400911 215,279,278,277,349,348,276,275,347,346,274,273,345,344,343,419
%N A400911 Squares visited by a chess king moving on the square spiral, always moving to an adjacent unvisited square whose number has the largest sum of digits; ties are resolved by choosing the lowest number.
%C A400911 The board is numbered by the standard square spiral, with 1 at the origin, 2 immediately to its right, and 3 above 2. The king starts at square 1. At each step it moves to one of the eight adjacent squares that has not previously been visited. Digit sums are taken in base 10.
%C A400911 The sequence is finite. The last term is a(2032) = 7540.
%H A400911 Bartlomiej Pawlik, <a href="/A400911/b400911.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2032</a>
%e A400911 Starting from a(2) = 9, the available adjacent squares are 2, 8, 10, 11, 23, 24 and 25. Their sums of digits are 2, 8, 1, 2, 5, 6 and 7, respectively, so the maximum is attained by 8. Hence a(3) = 8.
%t A400911 Module[{d=DeleteCases[Tuples[{-1,0,1},2],{0,0}],u,f,p={0,0},s={1},a,c},u[{x_,y_}]:=1+Which[y>=Abs[x],4y^2-y-x,-x>=Abs[y],4x^2-x-y,-y>=Abs[x],(4y-3)y+x,True,(4x-3)x+y];f[n_]:=Total[IntegerDigits[n]];While[True,c=Select[{u[#],#}&/@((p+#)&/@d),!MemberQ[s,#[[1]]]&];If[c=={},Break[]];a=First@SortBy[c,{-f[#[[1]]]&,#[[1]]&}];p=a[[2]];AppendTo[s,a[[1]]]];s]
%Y A400911 Cf. A214176, A333713, A335661, A335816, A335856, A400593, A400596, A400597, A400598, A400599, A400761.
%K A400911 nonn,base,walk,fini,full
%O A400911 1,2
